<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Multan (1818)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghan-sikh-wars">Afghan–Sikh Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> in 1818 was the capture of the city of Multan from its Afghan governor by the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> cite it as one of the conquests that extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> rule. It brought an important commercial and strategic city into the empire.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> had belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> took the province in 1752 and gave it to Shuja Khan, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a>. Bhangi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> chiefs held it from 1772 until Timur Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> recovered it in 1779 and placed Muzaffar Khan Sadozai in charge, who later became independent in practice. The city lay on routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and its walled town enclosed a fort with 46 towers.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> led or sent expeditions against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> from 1802 onward, each ending in a payment of tribute; in 1810 his army took the city and besieged the fort for about two months. By 1817 Muzaffar Khan was unwilling to go on paying, repaired the fort and laid in six months of provisions. Ranjit Singh chose a moment when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> government was disorganised and Wazir Fateh Khan was occupied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The army, given as 20,000 or 25,000 horse and foot with 12 batteries, marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in January 1818 under the nominal leadership of the sixteen-year-old Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharak-singh">Kharak Singh</a>, with Misr Diwan Chand in actual command and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh-nalwa">Hari Singh Nalwa</a> among its officers. Boats on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a> carried supplies. After taking Khangarh and Muzaffargarh, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> commanders rejected a fresh offer of tribute, drove Muzaffar Khan's levies back behind the walls and took the city in February.</p><p>The fort held out for months. Its garrison made sorties and threw down stones, burning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> and boiling oil; mining met countermining, and one assault cost the besiegers 1,800 men. Terms offered through Bhawani Das were refused by Muzaffar Khan's officers. The Zamzama gun was then brought up and opened two breaches near the Khizri Gate. Historians date the final assault to 2 June or 20 June 1818. Sadhu Singh Akali led Nihang troops through a breach, and Muzaffar Khan was killed fighting there with several of his sons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The city and fort were plundered, houses were burned, and thousands of civilians were massacred. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> ordered the plunder returned to the state treasury, and about five lakhs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>' worth was surrendered. The surviving members of the family were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and given maintenance. News of the fall reached Lahore on 29 June 1818, and Diwan Chand was rewarded with titles and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a>. The conquest removed Afghan authority from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and opened the road toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Multan (1818)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
